This performance test came from a comment by dirk on a jbuilder issue proposing
caching improvements:

  https://github.com/rails/jbuilder/issues/289#issuecomment-146000448
  https://gist.github.com/dirk/4048df31641f5dab1c67 (the script itself)

How this differs from the original
----------------------------------

The gist was written to A/B test jbuilder's cache-hit rendering cost (master vs
the proposed branch), jbuilder-only, with benchmark-ips. For that purpose it
cached the entire document body: one wrapper key around one cached array, with
caching always on and nothing rendered live.

The 2017 port kept that shape and extended it into a cross-library comparison
(rabl, turbostreamer with oj and wankel encoders, the analyzer harness). Used as
a comparison, whole-document caching degenerates: the fastest implementation is
the one that returns the cached blob with the least work, and a real app would
cache a fully-cacheable response above the view layer anyway -- conditional GET
(fresh_when / ETags), a CDN or reverse proxy, a low-level Rails.cache around the
payload, or action caching (actionpack-action_caching). Fragment caching is for
partially cacheable responses, so that is what this suite now models.

Current differences from the original script:

- Live values (generated_at, request_id, item_count) are rendered around the
  cached fragment, so every implementation has to assemble the response on
  every iteration; nothing can serve the whole document from cache.
- Caching is toggled by PERFORM_CACHING (see lib.rb and the Rakefile, which
  runs the suite both ways). It is set on ActionController::Base and on the
  render context together: rabl consults the former, turbostreamer and jbuilder
  the latter, and setting only one silently disables one library's caching.
- All implementations emit a byte-identical document (dates are a single
  pre-formatted string; each encoder would otherwise apply its own Time
  format), cache the same fragment under the same key, and do no file I/O in
  the measured loop (rabl runs with cache_sources enabled, as deployed apps
  effectively have via template caching).
